WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.199 --> 00:00:09.160
With the exception of humans and possibly
that one mole-rat, it’s weird to think of

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:12.879
living things as naked.

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:18.380
This is particularly true for microbes, who
often seem to be made up of well-arranged

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.110
squish that requires very little in the way
of ornamentation.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:28.540
But we have met a few members of the microcosmos
who have figured out how to brighten up their

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:35.820
exterior, like the silica frustules made by
diatoms that harden their walls.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:41.440
These structures don’t satisfy any kind
of internal, diatomaceous vanity, but rather

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.010
offer protection against things like UV light.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:46.260
And then, there are the amoebas.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:57.610
The classic amoeba of popular imagination
is generally considered—scientifically speaking—naked.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:03.880
Their pseudopodia, or “false feet,” transport
their shifting, unadorned bodies throughout

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:05.930
the microcosmos.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:10.539
But as we’ve discussed before, amoebas are
a polyphyletic group.

00:01:10.539 --> 00:01:15.190
We’ve grouped different species together
because in the early days of microscopy, they

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:20.560
seemed to resemble each other in a way that
suggested a close relationship.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:27.069
But with molecular tools that reveal the hidden
history encoded in their DNA, we’ve learned

00:01:27.069 --> 00:01:32.679
that these relationships are actually much
more distant, and that the term “amoeba”

00:01:32.679 --> 00:01:35.459
does not define a single taxonomic group.

00:01:35.459 --> 00:01:41.829
Rather, it’s a descriptive term, encompassing
organisms that exhibit that distinct, crawling

00:01:41.829 --> 00:01:45.299
“amoeboid movement” using its pseudopodia.

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:51.250
So, saying something is an amoeba is more
like saying something is a flyer than saying

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:52.689
it is a bird.

00:01:52.689 --> 00:01:58.840
Birds are all related to each other, but a
flyer might be a bird, might be a bat, might

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:02.299
be a ladybug, might be a pterodactyl.

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:09.050
But this distinction remains important as
we venture into the lives of testate amoebas,

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:13.880
the more modest compatriots of the naked amoeba.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:19.150
Much like amoebas as a whole, the grouping
of “testate amoeba” is a polyphyletic

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:25.799
one, lumping together amoebas who encase themselves
in a shell called the “test.”

00:02:25.799 --> 00:02:30.819
The test acts as protection from changes in
the environment, as well as from potential

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:32.010
predators.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:38.090
But it also contains a hole, or “aperture,”
from which the amoeba’s pseudopodia can

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:42.130
come out and do what it needs to do.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:45.739
Different testate amoebas assemble their shells
out of different materials.

00:02:45.739 --> 00:02:51.569
In some cases, the test is autogenic, meaning
that the amoeba produces its own building

00:02:51.569 --> 00:02:52.569
material.

00:02:52.569 --> 00:02:58.280
For example, the shell of this Arcella here
is made out of a single layer of what is called

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:04.550
“proteinaceous alveoli,” which is comprised
of a keratin-like protein.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:09.469
This shell is shaped like a dome, with a central
cavity that holds the aperture.

00:03:09.469 --> 00:03:15.859
The Arcella produces this shell during binary
fission, readying it for the newly produced

00:03:15.859 --> 00:03:17.120
daughter cell.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:23.909
Before starting division, it will collect
the protein building blocks inside its cytoplasm.

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:31.939
And as division starts, the Arcella will extend
its cytoplasm through the aperture to create

00:03:31.939 --> 00:03:38.319
a bud, where those building blocks then get
gathered and assembled and converted—first

00:03:38.319 --> 00:03:42.150
into the right materials, and then into the
right shape.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:48.159
And when the new test is done forming, the
cell itself begins to divide.

00:03:48.159 --> 00:03:52.760
The Arcella’s shell starts out colorless,
but with time, they may become colored with

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:56.129
accumulating iron and manganese.

00:03:56.129 --> 00:04:02.920
Other autogenic tests may be made up of different
materials, like silica or calcium carbonate.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:08.530
But there are also amoebas who gather the
materials to make their shells from their

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:13.890
environment, making what are called xenogeneic
tests.

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:19.660
One example of this are the Difflugia amoeba,
whose shells are composed of various materials

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:25.330
including quartz or even diatom frustules
that are stuck together using the organism’s

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:27.530
own special glue.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:34.950
Their tests, also called agglutinate shells,
come in a variety of shapes: elongated, lobed,

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:41.480
horned—there’s an elaborate set of descriptors
used to specify this beautiful morphology

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.720
different species take on.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:48.670
While some of their structures may appear
at first glance to be random, the aperture

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:54.360
in particular often shows a much more orderly
arrangement.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:59.771
Some species have a tooth-like pattern lining
the aperture, while others might have a more

00:04:59.771 --> 00:05:01.570
rounded structure.

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:07.680
At first glance, Difflugia might not seem
particularly dexterous given the hardened

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.760
shell they choose to live in.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:15.390
They survive largely on bacteria and algae
anyway, so nimbleness may not be their primary

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:16.750
concern.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:21.750
But they have still managed feats of agility
that have astounded their observer.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:28.790
In one case, a scientist documented a Difflugia
attacking a rotifer, the amoeba stretching

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:35.860
six long pseudopods through its aperture to
trap its prey by the feet.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:41.760
How it managed to detect and quickly snare
its prey remains a mystery.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:47.660
But of course, amoebas are always teaching
us just how deceiving appearances can be,

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:52.730
a lesson that is constantly reinforced as
they demonstrate the limitation of trying

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:59.160
to establish evolutionary relationships between
these organisms just by looking at their morphology.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.640
Amoebas are a polyphyletic group of blobby
organisms.

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:08.840
Testate amoebas are a polyphyletic group of
blobby organisms with a shell.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:14.850
And in 2012, scientists established that Difflugia,
which share this common characteristic of

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:21.770
how they make their shells and thus seem pretty
closely related, are in fact a polyphyletic

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:27.290
group of blobby organisms with a particular
kind of shell.

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:32.160
Even within this group, distinguishing between
species has been a major challenge because

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:37.340
it relies so heavily on our observations of
shell size and shape.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:42.650
Slight changes in shell structure might make
one species appear to be different.

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:48.130
But we see so little of the organism itself,
and thus we can’t readily rely on other

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:50.540
cellular features to distinguish them.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:57.000
It’s only with genetic techniques that we
can begin to unravel more about the creatures

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.160
inside the shell.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:04.760
And unraveling these things is important not
just to our understanding of amoeba, but also

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:07.950
how we study the world around us.

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:13.600
Testate amoeba are abundant and distributed
in waters well around the world, and they’re

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:17.420
quite sensitive to conditions like moisture
or pH.

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:22.600
Perhaps most importantly, their shells are
well-preserved, a rarity in the microbial

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:23.820
world.

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:29.710
And the accumulation and distribution of their
shells allows scientists to study not just

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:35.880
current testate amoeba populations, but also
ancient ones whose remains can reveal what

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:39.590
their environments may have looked like.

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:45.240
Even with the vagaries of establishing different
species and evolutionary relationships, testate

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:50.620
amoebas have been extremely useful paleobioindicators.

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:57.010
But learning more about their diversity and
taxonomy will sharpen the kind of inferences

00:07:57.010 --> 00:08:02.030
scientists working in this field can make,
providing more insight into both microbial

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:05.460
evolution and ecological change.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:12.310
For all their influence and long, storied
impact on our earth, microorganisms have a

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:14.750
pretty ephemeral vibe.

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:20.620
Their life cycles are often short, and when
they die, they usually leave behind very little.

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:28.500
While the legacy of ancient microbes encompasses
every level of life, the only traces of their

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:32.440
individual identities are encoded in DNA.

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:39.170
But sometimes, as with the testate amoeba,
they leave behind a little something extra.

00:08:39.170 --> 00:08:46.550
The shells that protect the amoeba while alive,
and remain evidence of their existence long

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:48.660
after they are dead.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:53.810
Perhaps no one mourns the amoeba, but we do
study them.

00:08:53.810 --> 00:09:00.370
And in doing so, they teach us many things,
including just how complicated such a seemingly

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:06.120
simple organism can be.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:11.190
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:12.190
us.

